# apn-autoconfig-lpac AT APDU allow list v1
#
# Which modems may be given an AT APDU channel at all.
#
# This file exists because of one upstream incident and one property of the LPA.
#
# The incident: a generic AT `CCHO` probe wedged a Sierra SIM until physical
# intervention. APDU probing is a capability and a quirk, never a harmless scan
# — the reasoning is in docs/plan-1.0.md and the rule is in the C1 gate.
#
# The property: lpac's AT backend sends `AT+CCHO=?`, `AT+CCHC=?` and `AT+CGLA=?`
# to whatever device it is handed, on connect, before it parses its own
# subcommand. It cannot be configured out of doing so. The refusal therefore
# cannot live in the LPA; it lives in `lpac-run`, which decides whether to start
# the LPA at all, and this file is what it decides against.
#
# Format, tab-separated:
#
#   <vendor_id>	<product_id>	<model>	<state>
#
# `vendor_id` and `product_id` are the USB identifiers as they appear in sysfs,
# lowercase hex without the `0x`.
#
# **No wildcard is accepted in either identifier, by design.** Every other quirk
# table in this project allows `*` so a rule can be widened; widening this one
# would reinstate exactly the generic probe it exists to prevent. A device that
# is not named here is refused, and "unknown" and "generic" are the same answer.
#
# `state` is evidence about this entry and nothing else. `candidate` means the
# entry is here because the model is documented to support the commands and this
# project intends to drive it; `hardware-validated` is written only after an AT
# APDU exchange has been observed on that model, on real hardware, and recorded
# in a router-test document. A `candidate` entry is permitted to be tried — that
# is what naming it is for — but it may not be reported as validated.
#
0e8d	7127	Fibocom FM350-GL	candidate
